    "set2-l2-q14-17": {
      "scriptHash": "3803009a6a0499dde4430956fa2e9162fc8ba05fc482f90823e30ff1ba8a9817",
      "sigVersion": 3,
      "bytes": 1619217,
      "durationSec": 101.15,
      "audioSha256": "85c13d1550a6f8cea26feca410362642bd5c6aa4f54159a898d9b9eac20f6f43",
      "stt": {
        "verdict": "WARN",
        "wer": 0.0293,
        "backend": "faster_whisper",
        "reason": "WER 2.9% — queue for a human listen",
        "transcript": "All right, so today we're going to discuss how caves form. Most caves, especially the really large ones, are created through a process called dissolution. Basically, this happens when slightly acidic water dissolves rock over thousands of years. Now, let me explain how this works. Rainwater absorbs carbon dioxide from the air and soil, which makes it mildly acidic, kind of like natural soda water. When this acidic water seeps into the ground, it moves through cracks and joints in limestone or similar rocks. Over time, the acid slowly dissolves the rock and these small cracks gradually become larger openings. This process is incredibly slow. We're talking about thousands or even millions of years. As the dissolution continues, the passages get bigger and bigger until you have what we recognize as caves. The water also creates interesting formations inside caves. When water drips from the ceiling, it leaves behind tiny mineral deposits. These deposits build up over time, forming stalactites hanging from the ceiling and stalagmites rising from the floor. Some caves even have underground rivers or lakes. These form when the water table is high enough to fill the lower chambers. However, caves are fragile environments. Human activity, like excessive tourism or pollution, can damage these formations that took millennia to create. Changes in groundwater levels, due to draw row 43-ut or excessive pumping, can also affect cave systems. That's why geologists work hard to study and protect these natural wonders.",
        "audioSha256": "85c13d1550a6f8cea26feca410362642bd5c6aa4f54159a898d9b9eac20f6f43",
        "scriptHash": "3803009a6a0499dde4430956fa2e9162fc8ba05fc482f90823e30ff1ba8a9817"
      }
    },
